# Local research/cannibalization scratch (never commit cloned fleet repos)
.research/

# Editors / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# Go
bin/

# Node
node_modules/

# Terraform state (never commit)
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform/
*.tfvars

# Dependency lock files are generated per-environment against the local
# provider cache; this repo's cache holds re-packed providers whose checksums
# differ from registry releases, so locks are not committed (regenerate at
# deploy time with `terraform init`).
.terraform.lock.hcl

# Verify-gate attestation artifacts (generated by make verify)
.verify/
governance/board/violations.jsonl

# Runtime surface state: the rollback overlay the console's surface reader
# consults (issue #802) is host state — an engaged rollback belongs to the
# running deployment, never to a commit.
.rollout/

# Claim locks are runtime state (the claim ledger itself is tracked evidence)
.board/locks/

# Claim EVENTS are runtime state too: one collision-proof file per event, written
# by the tracked dispatcher (governance/dispatch/claims.py, DEFAULT_CLAIMS_DIR).
# Every lane that follows AGENTS.md -- claim the issue, then gate -- writes one,
# so without this rule a claiming lane worktree is red on check-gitignore.
# The rule names the DIRECTORY (trailing slash), the precise form its siblings
# above use; it is NOT what protects the frozen pre-#170 ledger
# `.board/claims.jsonl` (MEASURED: `.board/claims` and `.board/claims/` both
# leave that file alone, because the pattern names `claims`, not `claims.jsonl`).
# What protects it is check-gitignore.sh check 4, which refuses any rule that
# hides a TRACKED file -- the blanket `.board/` or the glob `.board/claims*` that
# a "tidy-up" of this rule invites is exactly the regression it catches.
.board/claims/

# The out-of-epic pool rail is runtime state too (epic #707, lane F6/#721): it
# records which issues the active focus parked and is rewritten on every
# drain, so committing it would put a moving dispatch signal in the diff.
# The decision log is the branch/PR history; the rail is the working state.
.board/pool.jsonl

# Fleet steering mailboxes are runtime state (schema + standing directive are tracked)
.fleet/inbox/
.fleet/outbox/
.fleet/sent/
.fleet/done/
.fleet/slog.jsonl
# Brain-rung surfaces (operator orders, replies) and rung liveness: runtime state.
.fleet/brain/
.fleet/*.heartbeat.json
.fleet/*.lock
# Run registry (which loop is tracking which directive) + report dedupe: runtime state.
.fleet/runs/
.fleet/reported/
# Lane isolation records: runtime state, one file per minted session (issue #263)
.fleet/lanes/
# Lifecycle journals: durable per-item evidence of what was verified and merged
.fleet/lifecycle/
# Session heartbeats: one per running lane, swept by governance/reconcile (#304)
.fleet/sessions/
# Watchdog remedy ledger (tracked module: fleet/watchdog.py, issue #773): one
# record per rung holding its attempt count and escalation, plus the
# one-per-incident escalation artifacts. Runtime state, never committed.
.fleet/watchdog/
# Fleet monitor runtime output (tracked module: fleet/monitor.py)
.fleet/open-eye.log
.fleet/open-eye.heartbeat
# Per-rung capture logs: each rung's stdout+stderr, appended by the spawner
# (tracked modules: fleet/watchdog.py, fleet/control.py, fleet/console.py)
.fleet/brain.log
.fleet/sister.log
.fleet/monitor.log
# Fleet retention (tracked module: fleet/prune.py, issue #280): the telemetry
# ledger, every rotated generation of an append-only ledger, and the pruner's
# own cron log are all runtime state, never committed.
.fleet/runs.jsonl
# Per-run telemetry ledger (tracked module: fleet/telemetry.py, issue #219):
# runtime state, one JSONL record per run, never committed.
.fleet/telemetry.jsonl
.fleet/*.jsonl.*
.fleet/prune.log
.fleet/watchdog.log
# Runaway guard (tracked module: fleet/runaway.py, AO-GR-21): the persisted
# per-directive attempt counter and the terminal dead-letter artifact. A refusal
# is a state transition recorded here, not a reason to try again next tick, so
# both are working state the loop rewrites.
.fleet/attempts/
.fleet/dead-letter/
# Wave plans (tracked module: fleet/brain.py, WAVES): one JSON per parent issue,
# rewritten as the wave advances. fleet/monitor.py and fleet/report.py read them;
# the plan is working state, while the child issues it dispatches are the log.
.fleet/waves/
# The lifecycle/reconcile BoardReporter's dedupe ledger (tracked module:
# governance/lifecycle/report.py, DEFAULT_LEDGER_RELPATH): rewritten on every
# pass and keyed by finding fingerprint, so the local ledger moves as findings
# are filed and resolved.
.fleet/board-reports.json
# Rung artifacts of tracked modules: the runner preflight's hold
# (fleet/terminal.py, #733), the cron reconcile log (fleet/cron.py), the
# monitor's queue-liveness signal (fleet/monitor.py, #695, its one write) and the
# operator pause flag (fleet/terminal.py).
.fleet/runner-hold.json
.fleet/reconcile.log
.fleet/queue-liveness.json
.fleet/paused

# Local environment / secrets
.env
.env.*
